                <pb facs="0894" n="778" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg778"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/3149: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-08-02T15:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 2,
                                1923—3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>159. Referring to Harbin’s telegram July 31, 6 p.m.</p>
                <p>You may represent to the Chinese Government that so radical and apparently
                    unnecessary an alteration of the <hi rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> would
                    not only impose upon the Chinese Government the burden of justifying <pb
                        facs="0895" n="779" xml:id="pg779"/> such action by it as trustee (as
                    recognized by Washington Conference Resolution No. 13),<anchor n="83"
                        xml:id="d611-f1"/> but under present circumstances would appear most
                    inopportune as tending to create in the minds of the treaty Powers a doubt as to
                    China’s intentions with regard to the observance of its express obligations to
                    foreign interests.</p>
                <p>It is also suggested that joint representations in this sense might be made to
                    the Chinese Government by the Powers participating in the Washington
                    Conference.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
